{
 "slug":"how-to-price-a-job","tag":"Quoting",
 "c1":"#3730a3","c2":"#6366f1","oglabel":"Quoting",
 "ogline1":"How to","ogline2":"Price a Job","ogtag":"free quotes, no sign-up",
 "subject":"a notebook with calculations, calculator and coffee on a desk",
 "title":"How to Price a Job: Hourly, Fixed and Value-Based Pricing",
 "ogtitle":"How to Price a Job",
 "cardh2":"How to Price a Job",
 "carddesc":"Pricing too low is the fastest way to go broke while busy. Here is how to price a job using hourly, fixed and value-based methods &mdash; and cover your real costs.",
 "desc":"How to price a job for a service business: working out your true costs and target rate, when to use hourly vs fixed vs value-based pricing, and how to quote a profitable price with confidence.",
 "lead":"Pricing too low is the quickest way to stay busy and broke. A solid price starts from your <b>real costs and target income</b>, then uses the method that fits the job. Here is how to work it out.",
 "sections":[
  {"h2":"Start from your real costs","html":"<p>Add up what the work truly costs you: your time, materials, tools, software, travel, and a share of overheads like insurance and admin. Then add the <b>profit margin</b> you need to grow. If a price does not cover all of this, it is a loss dressed up as a sale.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Set a target hourly rate","html":"<p>Even for fixed pricing, know your hourly rate. Take the annual income you want, add costs, and divide by the <b>billable</b> hours you can realistically work (not all 2,000+ in a year). That floor stops you underquoting.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Choose a pricing method","html":"<ul><li><b>Hourly</b> &mdash; best when scope is uncertain; you bill for time spent.</li><li><b>Fixed price</b> &mdash; best when scope is clear; the client gets certainty and you keep the upside if you work efficiently.</li><li><b>Value-based</b> &mdash; price on the outcome&rsquo;s worth to the client, not your hours; powerful when the result drives real revenue for them.</li></ul>"},
  {"h2":"Quote with confidence","html":"<p>Present one clear price (or a small good/better/best set), state what it includes, and avoid apologising for it. If a client pushes back, adjust <b>scope</b>, not just the number &mdash; drop a deliverable rather than your rate.</p>"},
 ],
 "faq":[
  {"q":"Should I charge hourly or a fixed price?","a":"Charge hourly when the scope is unclear and could expand; charge a fixed price when the scope is well defined. Fixed pricing rewards efficiency and gives the client certainty, but only quote it once you understand the work."},
  {"q":"How do I work out my hourly rate?","a":"Take the yearly income you want, add your business costs, and divide by the hours you can realistically bill in a year. Remember that not every working hour is billable, so the divisor is lower than you think."},
  {"q":"A client says my price is too high &mdash; what do I do?","a":"Adjust the scope rather than simply cutting your rate. Remove a deliverable or reduce the quantity so the lower price still reflects the value and keeps you profitable."},
 ],
 "related":[{"href":"/en/estimate","label":"Create a quote"},{"href":"/en/blog/what-to-include-in-a-quote","label":"What to include in a quote"},{"href":"/en/blog/how-to-write-a-quote","label":"How to write a quote"}],
 "cta_href":"/en/estimate","cta_label":"Create a quote free",
},
